{"title":"Next steps","markdown":{"headingText":"Next steps","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-next-steps","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n```{python}\n#| include: false\nfrom cohort_common import *\nfrom newest import new_novas, n_scans, n_usable, n_both\n\ndev_usable_share = np.mean([usable(h, c, WATER_MIN, CENTRE_MIN) for *_, h, c in scan_records])\ndev_compared = rule_results(WATER_MIN, CENTRE_MIN)[\"CANCER vs NORMAL\"][1]\n```\n\n1. **Label the newest patients.** The newest `{python} len(new_novas)` patients have been put through the same water-peak rule, blind to labels:\n   `{python} n_usable` of `{python} n_scans` scans (`{python} f\"{n_usable / n_scans:.0%}\"`) are water-rich, and **`{python} n_both` patients** have\n   both breasts usable (@sec-new-patients). That usable rate is lower than the `{python} f\"{dev_usable_share:.0%}\"` in the main analysis. Once\n   labels are in, those patients can test the CANCER vs NORMAL result. Only `{python} dev_compared` of `{python} len(analysis_novas)` patients in\n   the main analysis ended up in the headline comparison, so this newest group will give a handful of pairs at best — enough to check the\n   direction, not to confirm the 83%.\n2. **Strengthen the baseline.** More NORMAL/NORMAL patients with usable scans in both breasts, to measure how different two healthy breasts are.\n3. **Keep the threshold fixed** when new labelled patients are added, rather than retuning it, so the peak at `{python} f\"{WATER_MIN:g}\"`\n   (@sec-limitations) is not carried forward.\n4. **Trial mapping for Human 2.0.** Run the surface-then-XRD fly-scan protocol (1 Hz detector, speed-set collection, dynamic speed on thicker\n   tissue) and check that a whole-breast map finishes within the 5-minute target (@sec-mapping).\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi